      "narration": {
        "text": "Monetary policy works through interest rates. Brambledon's loan rate rises from three percent to seven percent. Because borrowing costs rise, monthly repayments take more profit, so the firm postpones a new workshop. Customers face dearer credit too, and consumer spending falls. Lower rates would reverse both effects. The transmission runs from the rate, to the loan, to spending, then to orders and to jobs across the supply chain.",
