      "narration": {
        "text": "During a downturn Brambledon acts. Because orders fall and cash is tight, therefore it cuts overtime and delays expansion, so it protects cash. It changes product mix toward cheaper commuter bikes, negotiates supplier terms and targets students. Cutting costs preserves the firm, but deep cuts can damage quality and morale. A firm that keeps training may recover faster when growth returns. Stock is reduced, prices are held where possible, and new models are postponed until demand improves. The lesson is to match spending to the stage of the cycle.",
